<!-- FAQ -->
<section class="tw-section-ivory">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="text-center mb-5 tw-fade">
      <span class="tw-section-label">Common Questions</span>
      <h2>Before you reach out, <em style="color:var(--tw-gold);">here are some answers</em></h2>
      <hr class="tw-divider">
    </div>
    <div class="row g-4 tw-fade">
      <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="tw-approach-card h-100">
          <h4>How do I know if therapy is right for me?</h4>
          <p>If you are asking that question, it probably is. Therapy is not only for people in crisis. It is for anyone who wants to understand themselves better, work through something that feels stuck, or simply have a dedicated space to process life. You do not need to be at a breaking point to benefit from therapy.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="tw-approach-card h-100">
          <h4>Does insurance cover psychotherapy in Ontario?</h4>
          <p>Many extended health benefit plans and Employee Assistance Programs (EAPs) cover sessions with a Registered Psychotherapist. The coverage amount varies by plan. Check with your insurance provider before your first session to understand what you are entitled to. Shimul can provide receipts formatted for insurance submission.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="tw-approach-card h-100">
          <h4>What is the difference between a psychotherapist and a psychologist?</h4>
          <p>Both are regulated mental health professionals in Ontario. Psychologists typically have doctoral-level training and can provide psychological assessments and diagnoses. Registered Psychotherapists are regulated by CRPO and specialize in therapeutic treatment. For most people seeking talk therapy and emotional support, a Registered Psychotherapist is an excellent, and often more accessible, choice.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="tw-approach-card h-100">
          <h4>How long does therapy typically take?</h4>
          <p>There is no single answer. Some people find that six to ten sessions gives them what they need. Others benefit from longer-term ongoing support. The timeline depends entirely on your goals, the nature of what you are working through, and the pace that feels right for you. Shimul will revisit this with you regularly throughout your work together.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="tw-approach-card h-100">
          <h4>Is everything I say completely confidential?</h4>
          <p>Yes, with a small number of legally required exceptions. Shimul is bound by CRPO ethical guidelines and Ontario privacy law to maintain strict confidentiality. The only exceptions are situations where there is imminent risk of harm to yourself or someone else, or where disclosure is required by court order. These exceptions will be explained clearly in your intake documents.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="col-md-6">
        <div class="tw-approach-card h-100">
          <h4>What if I need to cancel or reschedule?</h4>
          <p>Life happens. Shimul simply asks for a minimum of 24 hours notice for cancellations or rescheduling. Sessions cancelled with less than 24 hours notice may be subject to the full session fee. This policy exists to respect the time of both Shimul and other clients who may need that appointment slot.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>


